<TopicPage slug="subjects/astronomy/intermediate/stellar-life-cycles">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Stars are not eternal — they are <strong>born in clouds, live by fusion, and die in ways
    determined almost entirely by their mass</strong>. The strangest rule in astronomy: the
    bigger the star, the shorter its life. The Sun burns steadily for 10 billion years; a
    20-solar-mass giant exhausts itself in 8 million — geologically, the blink of an eye.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the four-stage story, from cloud to corpse" widget="equationStep" config={stellarCfg} />

  <h2>The Hertzsprung–Russell diagram</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The main sequence</strong> is not a path but a weighing scale: a star's brightness and colour reveal its mass. Sitting on the diagonal = burning hydrogen, 90% of a star's life.</li>
    <li><strong>Red giants and white dwarfs</strong> live off the main sequence — swollen, cooling elderly stars and their dense exposed cores (a sugar-cube of white dwarf matter weighs a tonne).</li>
    <li><strong>Fusion as alchemy:</strong> hydrogen → helium → carbon → oxygen → … up to iron in the most massive stars. Iron is the dead end — fusing it costs energy, which is precisely why core collapse is catastrophic.</li>
    <li><strong>We are the debris:</strong> carbon, oxygen, iron and calcium in your body were assembled in stars and scattered by their deaths. "We are made of star-stuff" is a literal inventory.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> OpenStax, Astronomy 2e (Stellar Evolution); Kippenhahn, Stellar Structure and Evolution.</p>
</TopicPage>
